sec.p:
The aperiodic activity L was estimated using a Lorentzian function characterized by an offset b, exponent x, and knee k (Donoghue et al., 2020).:
(1)
L=b−log⁡(k+Fx) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} L=b-\log \left(k+F^{x}\right) \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
with F denoting frequency (Hz). We then transformed the knee to the knee frequency (fk) using the exponent (Donoghue et al., 2020):
(2)
fk=k1x \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f_{k}=k^{\frac{1}{x}} \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The knee frequency defines the frequency at which the power is no longer constant across frequencies, but instead decreases with increasing frequencies (Donoghue et al., 2020). Finally, using the knee frequency, we could estimate the decay time constant 𝜏 of each vertex (Gao et al., 2020):
(3)
τ=12πfk \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau=\frac{1}{2 \pi f_{k}} \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The decay time constant describes the rate at which the autocorrelation function, which quantifies the signal’s self-similarity, decreases over time. For each drug condition, we parcellated the decay time constants into the Desikan-Killiany atlas with 34 cortical regions per hemisphere by computing the median timescale of all vertices in each parcel (Desikan et al., 2006; Shafiei et al., 2023). As a validation check, we correlated our resulting timescale maps with the publicly available intrinsic timescale map (Shafiei et al., 2023; Van Essen et al., 2013).
